[{:db/id 17592186064044 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2d9fc36f-8d8f-5e57-9ff0-28a032e71980" :question/math-academy-id "e-3726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](images/08/0811362e235ec7c6e93a8bf892a451f8c347d0f633f251b9ed955062a53dd6c3.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true." :task-item/_content [{:db/id 17592186729511 :task-item/content {:db/id 17592186064044} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 3.745 :task-item/id #uuid "0acdb315-1048-4803-86d4-f37352c10cf1" :task-item/next {:db/id 17592186729512} :task-item/status {:db/ident :task-item.status/completed}}]} {:db/id 17592186064116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "97897d09-3dc9-5e35-8f56-d44f54c542cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6169" :question/problem "![](images/50/50581635273c185b9ca3aefa907b2e2a4981ec1bf6be1c687bec47591e126a89.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](images/56/56e798c0942ddcf0b1786afb054ab6dfbea7768c86751555808d86cc4cfa89f6.png)\n\n  \n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point." :task-item/_content [{:db/id 17592186729421 :task-item/content {:db/id 17592186064116} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 8.829 :task-item/id #uuid "662533b8-06d3-4ff3-9e6f-a506fe1d0a46" :task-item/next {:db/id 17592186729422} :task-item/status {:db/ident :task-item.status/completed}}]} {:db/id 17592186064800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c01ea863-d362-5b15-9ac1-40a760906ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id 17592186062858} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc57029-9f8d-5b61-a7fe-3f9d4be90930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id 17592186062859} {:answer/id #uuid "35898123-c6d1-5700-bb11-f25d11a69e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 1$" :db/id 17592186062860} {:answer/id #uuid "449252a8-5c4a-5d89-80a6-011c69e39ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 3$" :db/id 17592186062861} {:answer/id #uuid "7dded944-d0eb-59fc-b600-6926227f8f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ and $x = 6$" :db/id 17592186062862}] :answer-field/id 